+-- The HDR page (Far Cry RayMaster): XRenderD3D12's output to a display in HDR mode (Windows: Use
+-- HDR) and its brightness, opened by the button of HDR output in Advanced Video Options:
+--   HDR Output           d3d12_HDROutput: SDR, HDR10 or scRGB. The highlights of the scene (the
+--                        sun, fire, lamps) go up to the peak, the rest of the picture, the HUD and
+--                        the menu stay at SDR brightness. It needs the HDR pipeline: choosing HDR10
+--                        or scRGB while HDR is None sets HDR to Soft. On a display in SDR the output
+--                        stays SDR (the line next to it says what the display is).
+--   SDR Brightness       d3d12_HDRPaperWhite, the light of the game's white, the menu's and the
+--                        HUD's: Windows' SDR content brightness at the slider's left end (0), else
+--                        80 to 480 nits.
+--   Peak Brightness      d3d12_HDRPeak, the most light of the highlights: the display's at the
+--                        slider's left end (0), else 250 to 4000 nits.
+--   Frame Average Limit  d3d12_HDRFrameAverage: Display (1), the frame's average light at most the
+--                        display's full frame light, so that an OLED does not dim the picture
+--                        itself; Off (0).
+-- What is chosen applies at once (the renderer reads the variables every frame) and is saved when
+-- the page closes. While it is open the HDR output shows its test under the rows
+-- (d3d12_HDROutputDebug 3, in d3d12_HDROutputTestRect, whose default is the free area here): at
+-- the left a square at 10000 nits around a smaller one at the peak, which disappears into it once
+-- the peak is the display's; at the right patches at paper white, twice and four times that and
+-- at the peak. With another renderer (no d3d12_HDROutputState, which XRenderD3D12 does not save)
+-- the rows are greyed out.
